// Pull request: #6538 New Rule: Potential Ingress Tool Transfer Via ECMangen.EXE
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6538
// Pull request author: COMPUMAX-EC
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Ingress Tool Transfer Via ECMangen.EXE
// Sigma ID: f9b360f9-11a5-47af-875e-630c85820643
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Geovanny Basantes
// Date: 2026-10-28 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, T1105
// Description: Detects execution of "ECMangen.exe" with a remote URL on the command line. ECMangen.exe is a
//   utility that ships with the Windows SDK and with Microsoft Exchange Server. It does not take a
//   URL as part of its normal usage. When given a URL, it downloads the remote content and stores it
//   in the INetCache folder, which attackers can abuse to transfer tools onto a host.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/OtherMSBinaries/ECMangen/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/fae6393930944b92a19215e5e60499ecd11ad4f4/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_ecmangen_ingress_tool_transfer.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\ECMangen.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "ECMangen.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "http://" or ProcessCommandLine contains "https://")
